{
  "dom_id": "step-q124469",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14226474/assets/fb14c05b065e8f3745dd6f1f3ad793abef565dac91047a89663c588519eabaaf.png)\n\nFind the area (in square units) of $△ABC$ shown above.",
  "worked_solution": "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}BC\\cdot AB.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $m∠A={30}^{∘}.$ Notice that relative to $∠A,$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{BC}{AB} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘}) & =\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{3}\\,x+3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} & =\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{3}\\,x+3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3}(\\sqrt{3}\\,x+3) & =3\\cdot 2x \\\\ 3x+3\\sqrt{3} & =6x \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3} & =3x \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =2x=2\\sqrt{3}, \\\\ AB & =\\sqrt{3}\\,x+3=\\sqrt{3}\\,(\\sqrt{3})+3=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot BC\\cdot AB \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
